  "content": {
    "title": "When will the Chicago River turn green? Date, time, more details",
    "text": "March 13, 2026, 5:00 p.m. ETOne of the biggest cities in the United States will be filled with green this weekend, and not just by people wearing festive clothing.Chicago is set to once again dye its river green to celebrate St. Patrick's Day, continuing a longstanding tradition and transforming the waterway that runs through parts of the downtown area to a shade fit for a leprechaun.With thousands of spectators expected to line the city's streets to get a glimpse of the green river, here's what to know about the tradition ahead of the dyeing.When will the Chicago River be dyed green? See date, timeThe Chicago River dyeing is slated to take place on Saturday, March 14. The river dyeing is typically held on the Saturday before St. Patrick's Day.This year, the river dyeing starts at 10 a.m. and takes about 45 minutes to complete, with the color lasting a few hours to a couple of days, according to CBS News.Two motorboats mainly handle the dyeing of the river, which consists of one dumping the dye powder and another stirring the water to create the neon green effect as the crowd cheers them on.Why does Chicago dye its river?The river dyeing started in 1962 as a way to celebrate St. Patrick's Day in the city. It was kicked off by the Chicago Journeymen Plumbers Local Union, who were originally using green dye to locate pipes that were leaking sewage into the river’s waterfront areas at the request of then-Mayor Richard J. Daley, according to Enjoy Illinois.When they noticed the dye resembled the emerald color often linked to St. Patrick's Day, the union decided to pour 100 pounds of the dye into the Chicago River to celebrate the holiday, rendering the river green for a week. Daley initially wanted to dye Lake Michigan green, but eventually opted for the river instead.While the union originally colored the river with an oil-based dye that lingered in the water, Chicago has since switched to a more environmentally friendly powdered, vegetable-based dye after urging from environmentalists, according to The Smithsonian. However, the dye's formula is kept a secret.Tips if you're trying to see the Chicago River dyeing in personIf you're hoping to see the dyeing in person, arriving early and claiming your spot along the river will likely help you have a view of the green water.Specifically, outlets over the years have reported that the best viewing spots are between State and Columbus on Upper Wacker and across the bridges. The Lower Riverwalk is typically closed except for select ticketed events.Spectators can also view the dyeing from many of the nearby restaurants and bars that line the Chicago River, as well as from one of the river cruises that are often also on the water during or immediately after the event.When are the Chicago St. Patrick's Day parades?The celebrations don't stop with the green river. After the river dyeing, the 71st Chicago St. Patrick’s Day Parade will start downtown around noon.The parade will head north from Columbus Drive and Balbo. This year's theme is faith, peace, and unity, and ABC7 Chicago will broadcast the parade downtown.On Sunday, the South Side Irish St. Patrick's Day Parade will set off at noon from 103rd and Western Avenue.Why do we celebrate St. Patrick's Day?St. Patrick's Day originated as a religious day to celebrate the saint who introduced Christianity to Ireland in the 5th century.March 17, the day St. Patrick is believed to have died, is a day celebrating all things Irish. Approximately 9.5% of U.S. residents claimed Irish ancestry in 2024, according to the U.S. Census Bureau.While it is not a federal holiday in the United States, many bar-goers still don green attire, beads and glasses. In Ireland, it is a national holiday, and events kick off the tourist season.Contributing: USA TODAY Network's Kinsey Crowley and Hannah Hudnall",
